<section class="lsec rv " id="process" aria-labelledby="h-process"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[4] (steps) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>05 · Process</span></p><h2 id="h-process">My CRM Development Process</h2><p class="lead nar">CRM projects fail most often when discovery gets skipped and development starts before anyone has mapped the workflow. Here’s how engagements run from first call to production.</p>
 <ol class="hsteps" style="--n:5"><li><span class="sn">01</span><b>Discovery</b><p>I map user roles, lead sources, assignment rules, integrations and reporting needs before writing any code. This step alone catches most of the scoping mistakes that show up later.</p></li><li><span class="sn">02</span><b>Architecture</b><p>Database schema, tenant model (if the system needs to be multi-company), permission layers and API contracts, all documented upfront so there’s no ambiguity once development starts.</p></li><li><span class="sn">03</span><b>Development</b><p>Iterative sprints with working demos every 1–2 weeks: real lead routing and dashboards functioning, not wireframes or mockups standing in for progress.</p></li><li><span class="sn">04</span><b>Testing</b><p>Critical paths get tested specifically: authentication, lead assignment logic, data imports, payroll calculations (if HRM is involved), and every API contract defined during architecture.</p></li><li><span class="sn">05</span><b>Deployment &amp; handover</b><p>Production deployment, queue configuration, full documentation and a team walkthrough, so your team can operate the CRM after launch instead of depending on me for every small change.</p></li></ol></div>
</section>

